          %title Nabla
          %h1#nabla Nabla
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#historical_and_terminological_notes"} Historical and terminological notes
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The operator
            %strong nabla
            (also called
            %strong Atled
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %strong Del
            = succeed ")" do
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∇
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            = succeed "ic" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quaternion"} quaternion
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+analysis"} vector analysis
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gradient"} gradient
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curl"} curl
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/divergence"} divergence
            may all be defined in terms of it.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Informally, nabla is defined to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector"} vector
            (to be thought of as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+vector"} tangent vector
            on some
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+space"} cartesian space
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^3
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+operator"} differential operator
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo =
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mi k
                  %mfrac
                    %mo ∂
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi k
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla = \sum_{k = 1}^n e_k \frac{\partial}{\partial x^k} ,
          %p
            and it may be applied to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+field"} scalar field
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+field"} vector field
            in any way that it may be possible to multiply a scalar or vector (respectively) by a vector. Classically, this gives three applications: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gradient"} gradient
            of a scalar field (corresponding to scalar multiplication), the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/divergence"} divergence
            of a vector field (corresponding to the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+product"} dot product
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curl"} curl or rotation
            of a vector field (corresponding to the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cross+product"} cross product
          %p
            Formally, all uses may be combined into a single definition based on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integration
            on
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypersurface"} hypersurface
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is a smooth
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundles"} vector bundles
            over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊙
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \odot
            is a smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bilinear+operator"} bilinear operator
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent bundle
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is a smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo ⊙
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla \odot T
            is a smooth section of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            whose value at any point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is given by the integral formula
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo ⊙
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi lim
                    %mrow
                      %mi vol
                      %mi D
                      %mo →
                      %mn 0
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mi vol
                      %mi D
                  %msub
                    %mo ∮
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi D
                  %mover
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ⊙
                  %mi T
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi d
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mover
                    %mi D
                    %mo ∘
                  %mo ∋
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\nabla \odot T)(x) = lim_{vol D\to 0} \frac{1}{vol D} \oint_{\partial D} \vec{n} \odot T \,d S,\,\,\,\,\stackrel{\circ}{D}\ni x,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            runs over the regions with smooth boundary
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∂
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial{D}
            containing the point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{n}
            is the unit vector of outer normal to the surface
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo =
                    %mo ∂
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S=\partial D
          %p
            The formula does not depend (when everything in smooth) on the shape of boundaries taken in limiting process, so one can typically take a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate+chart"} coordinate chart
            containing balls
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = D_r
            with decreasing radius
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \gt 0
            in this particular coordinate chart. The formula also does not depend on
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} orientation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∂
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial{D}
            is naturally a
            %span.newWikiWord
              pseudo-oriented
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/pseudo-oriented+submanifold"} ?
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/submanifold"} submanifold
            and so
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo ⊙
                  %mi T
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi d
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{n} \odot T \,d S
            (also written
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mover
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                    %mo ⊙
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d\vec{S} \odot T
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudoform"} pseudo
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+form"} form
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            is a
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+bundle"} trivial bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            (where every vector bundle is trivial), and the limit is again independent of this choice.
          %p
            This formula can also be extended to the case where
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            itself, is only
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^k
            and not smooth, at the risk that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo ⊙
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla \odot T
            might not be defined or that the choice of coordinate chart might make a difference even in the limit. In that case, we consider that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo ⊙
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla \odot T
            is defined only if the choice of coordinate chart in fact makes no difference.
          %h2#historical_and_terminological_notes Historical and terminological notes
          %p
            The nabla is an old instrument in the shape of inverted triangle, sort of an Assyrian harp.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Hamilton"} William Hamilton
            introduced the operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            (sometimes called Hamilton’s or Hamiltonian operator, but distinguish strictly from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            in the mechanics sense) and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+Maxwell"} James Maxwell
            used it to write
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxwell%27s+equations"} his equations
          %ul
            %li
              History of nabla
              %a{:href => "https://arnold-neumaier.at/contrib/nabla.txt"} nabla.txt
          %p
            The terms ‘Atled’ and ‘Del’ are based on the idea that the nabla is an upside-down capital Greek Delta. Sometimes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+derivative"} partial derivative
            symbol
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∂
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial
            is taken to be the lowercase equivalent, as it is a variant (but not upside-down) of the lowercase Delta.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong nabla
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gradient"} gradient
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gradient+flow"} gradient flow
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+gradient"} symplectic gradient
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+flow"} Hamiltonian flow
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curl"} curl
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/divergence"} divergence
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              G.E. Shilov,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Mathematical analysis (functions of several real variables)
